  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Dreyfoos studied engineering at MIT, served in the United States Air Force and earned an MBA from Harvard. After work at Technicolor and IBM, he co-founded Photo Electronics Corporation in 1963. Its Video Color Negative Analyzer improved colour-print production, reached laboratories worldwide through Kodak and produced a motion-picture version recognised by the Academy of Motion Picture Arts and Sciences.\n\nThe success of Photo Electronics and his ownership of television station WPEC gave Dreyfoos the resources for sustained civic investment. He helped form Palm Beach County’s cultural council, led the campaign for the Kravis Center and supported the public arts school that bears his name. His later philanthropy advanced neuroscience research at the Max Planck Florida Institute and supported the scientific community around Palm Beach County.",
    "html": "<p>Dreyfoos studied engineering at MIT, served in the United States Air Force and earned an MBA from Harvard. After work at Technicolor and IBM, he co-founded Photo Electronics Corporation in 1963. Its Video Color Negative Analyzer improved colour-print production, reached laboratories worldwide through Kodak and produced a motion-picture version recognised by the Academy of Motion Picture Arts and Sciences.</p>\r\n<p>The success of Photo Electronics and his ownership of television station WPEC gave Dreyfoos the resources for sustained civic investment. He helped form Palm Beach County’s cultural council, led the campaign for the Kravis Center and supported the public arts school that bears his name. His later philanthropy advanced neuroscience research at the Max Planck Florida Institute and supported the scientific community around Palm Beach County.</p>\r\n",
